import type { PointKey, ZodiacSign } from "../types";

export const PLANET_IN_SIGN_EN: Partial<Record<PointKey, Record<ZodiacSign, string>>> = {
  sun: {
    belier: "The Sun in Aries operates through momentum: identity proves itself through action, through the ability to initiate, charge ahead, be first — vital energy needs a field of conquest to fully exist. The inner tension shows up in the aftermath: once the obstacle is cleared or the novelty fades, interest can collapse as fast as it rose, and the ego struggles to find its footing when there's nothing left to conquer. The growth edge is learning that self-worth isn't measured only by the speed of the first reaction, and that seeing a project through builds a steadier confidence than momentum alone.",
    taureau: "The Sun in Taurus builds identity through slow accumulation: one exists by holding, stabilizing, giving value to what lasts — every concrete gain (a place, a skill, a relationship) becomes a pillar of who one is. The tension lies in change: what reassures can also confine, and the fear of losing what's been built sometimes holds back evolutions that are actually needed. What matters most here is learning that a form can be released without losing the value it held — that inner security doesn't depend solely on what stays identical.",
    gemeaux: "The Sun in Gemini defines itself through the movement of the mind: identity is built by connecting, commenting, moving from one subject to another — to exist is to be in dialogue with the world rather than fixed on a single truth. The friction shows up in coherence: the constant need for novelty can give the impression of someone who changes opinion or face depending on the audience, and deep commitment to one subject or person can feel like it threatens the freedom to circulate. The real work is discovering that going deep isn't the same as being trapped — that curiosity and a stable center to return to can coexist.",
    cancer: "The Sun in Cancer builds identity from connection: one exists by protecting, remembering, belonging to a family or emotional story that precedes and outlives the individual. The sensitive spot sits in exposure: asserting oneself publicly or claiming space in the open can clash with the fear of being hurt or rejected, so the protective shell meant to guard what's precious ends up smothering its expression instead. The lesson to take in is learning that security can also be built by stepping into the open, not only by retreating — that the outside world isn't automatically a threat to what matters inside.",
    lion: "The Sun is at home in Leo: identity is built through radiance, creation, the need to exist in others' eyes with flair — being seen isn't shallow vanity but an almost vital condition for self-esteem. The shadow side lives in dependence on that gaze: when recognition is missing, confidence can collapse far faster than the displayed self-assurance suggests, and pride makes it hard to ask for help or admit a flaw. What this placement asks for is learning to draw self-worth from within as much as from without, so radiance becomes a gift rather than a constant hunt for approval.",
    vierge: "The Sun in Virgo builds itself through usefulness: identity proves itself in work well done, in precision, in the ability to improve what already exists — self-esteem almost always runs through a service rendered or a skill exercised. What costs the most plays out in self-judgment: standards aimed outward turn just as easily inward, and the sense of never doing quite enough can erode confidence even when the results are there. The deeper task is learning that self-worth isn't measured only by what's been corrected or perfected — that one is allowed to exist without being flawless.",
    balance: "The Sun in Libra builds itself in the mirror of the other: identity is clarified and confirmed through relationship, exchange, the gaze of a counterpart — existing alone, without another viewpoint to measure against, can feel incomplete. The knot sits in self-assertion: the constant concern for balance and not displeasing can lead to silencing one's own desires, until what one actually wants gets lost behind what one assumes the other expects. What's being cultivated here is learning to decide and to disappoint without losing the other's love — that lasting harmony sometimes requires an honest disagreement.",
    scorpion: "The Sun in Scorpio builds identity through intensity and control: one exists by going to the bottom of things, refusing superficiality, with an almost vital need to understand what's really happening beneath the surface — in oneself as much as in others. The main difficulty lies in trust: the fear of being betrayed or overpowered pushes toward keeping control, testing the other before opening up, which can isolate as much as it protects. The place to grow is learning that real power isn't only in control, but also in the capacity to be vulnerable without that meaning defeat — that transformation, once gone through, tends to free more than it threatens.",
    sagittaire: "The Sun in Sagittarius builds itself through the search for meaning: identity unfolds by exploring, by believing in something larger than the everyday, with a genuine need for horizon and movement to stay oneself. The downside shows up in staying power: enthusiasm for what's new makes the initial commitment easy but can make it hard to sustain once the novelty and promise of adventure fade. The growth edge is learning that freedom doesn't have to oppose commitment — that one can explore a single territory deeply instead of multiplying horizons to escape routine.",
    capricorne: "The Sun in Capricorn builds identity over time: one exists by carrying responsibility, by climbing patiently, with a need to earn one's place rather than receive it freely. The tension lies in lightness: an early-onset seriousness, often rooted in a need to prove reliability young, can make it hard to rest, to play, to receive without having to earn it through effort. What matters most here is learning that authority and success don't need to be fought for at every moment — that control can be loosened without losing what's been built.",
    verseau: "The Sun in Aquarius builds identity through deliberate difference: one exists by thinking differently, refusing to fit the mold, with a real need to belong to something larger than oneself while remaining irreducibly singular. The friction shows up in intimacy: the distance that protects independence can also hold others further away than one would actually like, and raw emotion is sometimes harder to own than an idea. The real work is learning that closeness doesn't erase singularity — that one can belong to a bond without dissolving into it.",
    poissons: "The Sun in Pisces builds a fluid, permeable identity, existing by connecting to something vaster — an inspiration, a cause, a collective emotion — rather than by asserting a clearly bounded outline. The sensitive spot sits in boundaries: empathy and porousness make connection easy but can blur where the self ends and the other begins, at the risk of dissolving into others' needs or emotions. The lesson to take in is learning to set limits without closing off — that sensitivity can remain a gift even once channeled by a clearer shape.",
  },
  moon: {
    belier: "The Moon in Aries reacts before it reflects: the need for security translates into immediate energy, into action or words rather than withdrawal — emotion discharges fast, which keeps it from building up but also leaves little room for introspection. The shadow side lives in vulnerability: the speed of the reaction protects from fully feeling what's difficult, and anger or impatience often mask a deeper hurt or fear that hasn't had time to be named. What this placement asks for is learning to slow down just enough to sense what's behind the immediate reaction, without losing the spontaneity that is also a real strength.",
    taureau: "The Moon in Taurus is soothed by the sensory and the stable: the need for emotional security runs through the body, through routine, through the reassuring presence of what doesn't change — comfort is almost physical before it's verbal. What costs the most plays out in change: what soothes can also become resistance, an attachment so strong to familiar habits and people that any disruption, even a positive one, is felt as a threat. The deeper task is learning that inner security can survive a change of scenery — that stability can also be built within, not only around oneself.",
    gemeaux: "The Moon in Gemini processes emotion through words and thought: naming, telling, analyzing what's felt is often the only way to make an emotion bearable, as if putting it into language makes it more manageable. The knot sits in raw feeling: this constant mental mediation can create distance from the emotion itself, to the point of describing it well without ever truly moving through or digesting it. What's being cultivated here is learning to sit with a silent emotion, without needing to put it into words right away to make it legitimate.",
    cancer: "The Moon is at home in Cancer: sensitivity is direct, unfiltered, with an emotional memory that keeps the trace of every important bond — the need for a nest, for belonging, for protection is a central, almost instinctive drive. The main difficulty lies in dependence on the past: that vivid memory can replay old wounds in the present, coloring a new situation with fears from a history that no longer really applies. The place to grow is learning to tell apart what belongs to the past from what's actually happening today, so memory protects without imprisoning.",
    lion: "The Moon in Leo needs to love and be loved with flair: emotional security is built through visible recognition, through clearly mattering to someone rather than through a quiet, unspoken bond. The downside shows up in pride: asking for help or showing raw vulnerability can feel humiliating, so pain often hides behind a facade of strength or dignity that keeps others from truly getting close. The growth edge is learning that owned vulnerability, far from diminishing, can actually deepen the bond and the admiration so deeply sought.",
    vierge: "The Moon in Virgo calms anxiety through useful action: tidying, organizing, being of service, fixing a detail — keeping hands and mind busy becomes a way of managing an emotion that, expressed directly, feels too messy or too exposed. The tension lies in self-criticism: that same standard turned on oneself can turn ordinary worry into a chronic sense of never doing or being quite enough. What matters most here is learning to receive comfort without having to earn it through service, and to accept emotional mess without rushing to fix it.",
    balance: "The Moon in Libra needs relational harmony to feel safe: the emotional climate around it matters almost as much as what's happening inside, and tension or conflict is felt as a genuine inner threat. The friction shows up in authenticity: this need to soothe and please can lead to silencing a real disagreement or a negative emotion to preserve the peace, at the risk of it building up quietly until it comes out disproportionately. The real work is learning that expressing an honest disagreement doesn't necessarily break the bond — it can actually deepen it.",
    scorpion: "The Moon in Scorpio holds nothing back: emotions are felt at full intensity, often in silence, with an almost instinctive radar for what's hidden, unspoken or manipulated in others — a real emotional radar, paired with a vulnerability rarely shown. Once given, attachment is lived as total commitment: this is a deeply loyal Moon, capable of unwavering fidelity toward whoever has earned its trust, and for whom betrayal — real or only perceived — leaves a lasting mark, almost impossible to fully erase. The sensitive spot sits in trust: the fear of being betrayed or hurt pushes toward keeping control, testing the other's loyalty before letting the guard down, which can create emotional loneliness even inside a close relationship — the need for emotional truth is real, but it comes with real difficulty believing one can be loved unconditionally, without a test to pass first. The lesson to take in is learning that opening up without a guarantee isn't the same as losing control — that real strength isn't mastering everything, but allowing oneself to be seen whole, fragility included, without it becoming a weapon in someone else's hands. This is also a Moon that metabolizes crisis better than almost any other: what passes through it changes it deeply, rarely just on the surface.",
    sagittaire: "The Moon in Sagittarius finds its security in movement and space: the emotional need is satisfied as much by the freedom to move, explore, believe in something bigger, as by being anchored in one place or a fixed routine. The shadow side lives in commitment: the fear of feeling trapped can trigger flight at the first sign of relational weight, sometimes confusing a need for air with a genuine need to leave. What this placement asks for is learning that one can stay committed while keeping room to breathe — that inner freedom doesn't depend solely on the freedom to leave.",
    capricorne: "The Moon in Capricorn protects itself through self-control: emotion is lived privately, rarely shown, with a reserve that often comes from having learned early to appear solid rather than be comforted or reassured. What costs the most plays out in asking: it's hard to ask for help or comfort, as if doing so would reveal an unacceptable weakness, so a deep emotional need often stays invisible even to those closest. The deeper task is learning that receiving support doesn't cancel out competence or strength — that emotional security built late can, once earned, become some of the most solid there is.",
    verseau: "The Moon in Aquarius experiences emotion with mental distance: understanding and analyzing what's felt often comes before fully living it, and the need for freedom stays real even in the closest bonds — friendship and independence sometimes matter as much as fusion. The knot sits in closeness: this protective distance can be read by others as detachment or a lack of investment, when it's mostly a way of managing an emotional intensity that feels destabilizing. What's being cultivated here is learning to let oneself be touched without losing independence — that intimacy doesn't require giving up inner freedom.",
    poissons: "The Moon in Pisces feels everything, sometimes with no filter between what comes from within and what comes from others: empathy is immediate, almost instinctive, with a real need for retreat, daydreaming or spirituality to find oneself again after absorbing ambient emotion. The main difficulty lies in boundaries: this porousness can lead to getting lost in others' needs, confusing compassion with self-sacrifice, until it's unclear what one actually feels versus what's been absorbed from someone else. The place to grow is learning to set a limit without guilt, and to tell apart the emotion that's truly one's own from the one that's been picked up along the way.",
  },
  mercury: {
    belier: "Mercury in Aries thinks in reactive mode: the mind grabs a piece of information and decides almost instantly, with a quickness that gives a real edge in debate or fast decision-making. The downside shows up in nuance: deciding fast leaves little room to fully examine a question, and the conviction on display can outrun conclusions that haven't actually been thought through. The growth edge is learning to slow down just enough to check a first impression before defending it to the hilt — without losing the speed that remains a real asset.",
    taureau: "Mercury in Taurus thinks with deliberate slowness: an idea needs time to settle, to be tested against the concrete, before it's truly adopted — but once formed, it becomes an almost unshakeable conviction. The tension lies in flexibility: this mental solidity can turn into closed-mindedness, a refusal to consider a new argument simply because it disturbs an already settled opinion. What matters most here is learning that reconsidering an idea isn't weakness — that solidity and openness can coexist.",
    gemeaux: "Mercury is in its element here: thought moves by quick association, jumping easily from one subject to another, and the pleasure of verbal contact and fast learning is almost a vital need. The friction shows up in depth: this agility can prevent staying on a subject long enough to truly master it, and words can sometimes outrun a thought that hasn't fully formed yet. The real work is learning to consciously choose where to invest sustained focus, so curiosity sometimes leads to real expertise rather than always staying at the surface.",
    cancer: "Mercury in Cancer thinks through the filter of memory and emotion: information is never processed coldly — it gets tied to a lived experience, an atmosphere, a family feeling that colors it. The sensitive spot sits in objectivity: this intuitive mode of thinking can bring old fears back into a present line of reasoning, blurring the line between what's actually at stake and what merely echoes a past wound. The lesson to take in is learning to tell when a mental reaction belongs to the present and when it's replaying a memory, without dismissing the richness of this intuitive intelligence.",
    lion: "Mercury in Leo thinks to convince: expression needs to make an impression, to tell a story with conviction, and the idea itself often becomes an extension of the identity being defended. The shadow side lives in listening: this firm conviction can make it hard to really hear an opposing view without experiencing it as a personal challenge. What this placement asks for is learning to separate the strength of an idea from the pride carrying it — so opinions can change without feeling like losing face.",
    vierge: "Mercury is also very much at home in Virgo: the mind analyzes with precision, spots the detail that's off, and always prefers what's verifiable to what stays approximate. What costs the most plays out in self-criticism: that same critical eye turned inward can turn a normally rigorous mind into anxious rumination, unable to settle for a conclusion that's simply \"good enough.\" The deeper task is learning that exactness isn't always necessary to move forward — that methodical doubt has a point past which it becomes paralyzing.",
    balance: "Mercury in Libra thinks by systematically weighing pros and cons: every idea gets examined against its opposite, in a constant search for balance and diplomatic phrasing. The knot sits in deciding: this ongoing concern for seeing both sides of an argument can make it hard to take a clear stand, favoring consensus-friendly speech over an actual position. What's being cultivated here is learning that a firm opinion isn't necessarily unfair — that one can pick a side without losing the ability to understand the other.",
    scorpion: "Mercury in Scorpio thinks by digging beneath the surface: the mind is never satisfied with a surface-level explanation, always hunting for the subtext, the hidden motive, what isn't said out loud. The main difficulty lies in suspicion: this psychological sharpness can tip into constant wariness, assuming hidden agendas even where none exist. The place to grow is learning to use this insight as a tool for understanding rather than as a default defensive weapon — to trust sometimes without giving up discernment.",
    sagittaire: "Mercury in Sagittarius thinks big: the mind prefers the big picture, the broad idea or philosophical conviction, over details that feel secondary, with a direct frankness that goes straight to the point. The downside shows up in precision: this preference for the wide view can lose important nuances, and frankness can land as blunt without meaning to. The growth edge is learning that detail isn't the enemy of vision — that one can hold a broad conviction while still taking the time to check what it's actually built on.",
    capricorne: "Mercury in Capricorn thinks with method: the mind gets to the point, structures its ideas before expressing them, and orients its thinking almost always toward concrete, lasting application. The tension lies in spontaneity: this rigor can suppress the expression of a still-vague idea or an unrationalized feeling, as if only a fully-formed thought deserves to be shared. What matters most here is learning to think out loud, without needing every idea to already be solid and proven before voicing it.",
    verseau: "Mercury in Aquarius thinks outside the established frame: the mind likes to imagine the future differently, question what seems settled, with a real capacity for abstraction and systemic vision. The friction shows up in emotional grounding: this distanced way of thinking can become a way of avoiding feeling, treating a personal question as a purely theoretical problem to solve. The real work is learning to let an idea stay connected to what it means humanly, without reducing it to a cold, detached concept.",
    poissons: "Mercury in Pisces thinks by association and imagery rather than linear logic: intuition often comes before the rational argument, and an idea can be born from a feeling before it's even put into words. The sensitive spot sits in factual precision: this fluid thinking, valuable for creativity, doesn't lend itself well to rigorous exactness, and concrete details can blur or get lost along the way. The lesson to take in is learning to give a clear shape to an accurate intuition, without betraying it by forcing it into a logic that doesn't fit.",
  },
  venus: {
    belier: "Venus in Aries desires through spontaneous impulse: attraction sparks fast, almost instinctively, and the chase itself — the game of seduction, the thrill of pursuit — is often as exciting as the relationship that follows. The shadow side lives in staying power: this energy that ignites easily can fade just as fast once the goal is reached, making long-term commitment less natural than the initial spark. What this placement asks for is learning to rediscover, once the chase is won, a new form of desire — the desire to keep choosing the other person, day after day, rather than looking for the next spark elsewhere.",
    taureau: "Venus is in its element here: desire expresses itself through the senses, shared comfort, a loyalty and attachment that build slowly but, once anchored, hold with rare solidity. What costs the most plays out in change: this attachment can turn into possessiveness or resistance to any evolution in the bond, out of fear of losing what took so long to build. The deeper task is learning that emotional security can survive a change within the relationship — that loving deeply doesn't require freezing everything in place.",
    gemeaux: "Venus in Gemini desires through exchange: seduction happens through words, wit, the ability to surprise and never bore — the bond feeds on conversation as much as physical contact. The knot sits in consistency: this constant need for variety and stimulation can make relational routine hard to bear, even when it isn't actually boring. What's being cultivated here is learning to find novelty inside a stable relationship, rather than always seeking it elsewhere.",
    cancer: "Venus in Cancer desires merging: affection seeks security in the bond, the feeling of building a family with the other person, a protective tenderness expressed as much through care as through desire. The main difficulty lies in fear of loss: this deep attachment can tip into possessiveness or emotional dependence, where the idea of abandonment becomes more frightening than the bond itself is nourishing. The place to grow is learning that emotional security can also come from within — that loving without clinging makes the bond freer, not more fragile.",
    lion: "Venus in Leo desires with flair: seduction is generous, even theatrical, and the pleasure of loving always comes with a need to be admired in return — one loves big and wants to be loved just as big in return. The downside shows up in pride: this need for recognition can make it hard to accept romantic disagreement or rejection without experiencing it as a wound to the ego. The growth edge is learning that love doesn't need constant validation through the other's admiration to be real.",
    vierge: "Venus in Virgo expresses desire through service: affection is understated, expressed through concrete gestures, attention to detail, a concern for doing right by the other person rather than grand declarations. The tension lies in self-devaluation: this same perfectionism applied to the relationship can create a sense of never doing enough, or difficulty receiving without feeling indebted. What matters most here is learning to receive love without having to earn it through service, and to accept that an imperfect relationship is still a valid one.",
    balance: "Venus is also very much at home in Libra: desire seeks harmony, balance as a pair, a partnership where charm and relational aesthetics matter almost as much as the feeling itself. The friction shows up in self-assertion within the couple: this need for balance can lead to silencing one's own desires to preserve the peace, until what one actually wants gets lost behind what one assumes the other expects. The real work is learning that a real disagreement, honestly expressed, doesn't destroy harmony — it can make it more genuine.",
    scorpion: "Venus in Scorpio desires with exclusivity: attachment is never satisfied with the superficial, always seeking total fusion, emotional truth, a commitment that leaves no room for lukewarm or incidental. The sensitive spot sits in control: this intensity can tip into jealousy or possessiveness, especially when trust in the other isn't yet fully earned. The lesson to take in is learning that the intensity of a feeling doesn't need total control over the other person to stay real — that freely given trust makes the bond stronger, not more vulnerable.",
    sagittaire: "Venus in Sagittarius desires space: affection needs to share ideas, adventures, a common horizon, with a real reluctance toward anything resembling a routine cage. The shadow side lives in commitment: this need for freedom can be mistaken, by oneself or by the other, for difficulty truly investing for the long haul. What this placement asks for is learning that one can commit fully while still keeping room to breathe — that freedom and loyalty to a bond aren't necessarily at odds.",
    capricorne: "Venus in Capricorn desires seriously: commitment builds progressively, on concrete proof rather than the emotion of the moment, and seduction itself often takes a back seat to the patient construction of the bond. What costs the most plays out in spontaneity: this emotional caution can delay expressing desire or vulnerability, out of fear of exposing oneself before being certain the bond will hold. The deeper task is learning that a desire expressed early isn't necessarily a fragile one — that lightness has its place even in an attachment built to last.",
    verseau: "Venus in Aquarius desires freely: affection favors friendship, independence and originality within the bond, with real difficulty accepting routine or possessiveness as proof of love. The knot sits in emotional closeness: this protective distance can be read by the other as a lack of investment, even when the attachment is real but expressed differently. What's being cultivated here is learning that emotional closeness doesn't erase independence — that one can be close without merging.",
    poissons: "Venus in Pisces desires merging into an ideal: affection is romantic, empathetic, capable of full devotion to the other and of turning a relationship into something almost sacred. The main difficulty lies in clear-sightedness: this capacity to idealize can obscure who the other person really is, and self-sacrifice can end up valued more than respecting one's own limits. The place to grow is learning to love without erasing oneself — to keep a clear-eyed view of the other without losing the capacity for wonder that makes this love so generous.",
  },
  mars: {
    belier: "Mars is in its element here: energy expresses itself in a straight line, with a real need to take on challenges and act before even finishing the thought behind it. The downside shows up in staying power: this explosive vitality can burn out as fast as it ignited, and impatience can sabotage a goal that needed persistence rather than a first burst of energy. The growth edge is learning to channel this energy across an entire project, not just its launch — without dimming what makes it powerful.",
    taureau: "Mars in Taurus engages slowly but with formidable endurance: action starts after a delay, but once launched, it doesn't stop easily, carried by an almost unshakeable determination. The tension lies in anger: it takes a long time to build, which can look like great patience, but once it finally erupts, it's hard to calm and takes just as long to fade. What matters most here is learning to express frustration earlier, in small doses, rather than letting it build up to a breaking point.",
    gemeaux: "Mars in Gemini acts through words and mental agility rather than physical force: debate, quick argument, the right word at the right moment are its real weapons. The friction shows up in focus: this need to vary fronts of action can scatter an energy that would benefit from concentrating on one goal at a time, risking many starts and few finishes. The real work is learning to pick one battle and see it through, even when a more exciting new idea shows up along the way.",
    cancer: "Mars in Cancer fights indirectly, more defensively than offensively: energy activates mainly to protect loved ones or a threatened emotional territory, rarely to attack on neutral ground. The sensitive spot sits in expressing anger directly: it struggles to come out head-on and often passes through sulking, withdrawal or hints rather than open confrontation. The lesson to take in is learning to state a disagreement clearly, in the moment it happens, rather than letting it simmer silently until it comes out sideways.",
    lion: "Mars in Leo asserts itself with flair: energy needs to be seen succeeding, action always comes with a bit of self-staging, and real courage mixes with an ego chasing its share of glory. The shadow side lives in public failure: this proud energy struggles to be wrong in front of witnesses, and the fear of losing face can hold back a risk that actually needed taking. What this placement asks for is learning that an admitted failure doesn't diminish one's worth — that one can be wrong without losing one's shine.",
    vierge: "Mars in Virgo acts through method rather than brute force: energy applies itself to detail, to concrete efficiency, genuinely more concerned with doing it right than doing it fast. What costs the most plays out in self-criticism: that same perfectionism turned inward can stall momentum before it even starts, out of fear of not being up to the task. The deeper task is learning to act with a method that's \"good enough\" rather than perfect, so that high standards don't end up blocking action itself.",
    balance: "Mars in Libra fights through strategy and diplomacy rather than direct confrontation: energy always looks for the most elegant, fairest way to get what it wants without breaking the relationship. The knot sits in taking action: this preference for measured response can turn into indecision, a tendency to stall well past the point where a clear stand was needed. What's being cultivated here is learning that a disagreement clearly owned, even if unwelcome for a while, is usually healthier than a conflict avoided that just keeps simmering.",
    scorpion: "Mars is traditionally very much at home in Scorpio: determination runs deep, almost unshakeable, with a rare capacity for resistance and counterattack, and a pulsional energy — sexual as much as combative — that runs strong. The main difficulty lies in control: this intensity can turn into a power strategy or a need to master every situation, even ones where letting go would actually work better. The place to grow is learning that real power doesn't always need to prove itself through victory or total control of the situation.",
    sagittaire: "Mars in Sagittarius commits with enthusiasm to causes or ideals: energy needs meaning and wide-open space to unfold, with a directness that can come across as blunt. The downside shows up in staying power: this initial enthusiasm can fade once the novelty wears off, leaving a half-finished project in favor of a more exciting new horizon. The growth edge is learning to hold a commitment even once the initial spark has faded — that true freedom includes the freedom to stay.",
    capricorne: "Mars in Capricorn builds for the long haul: energy targets concrete, long-term goals, with a patient discipline that favors methodical construction over impulsiveness. The tension lies in lightness of action: this patient ambition can lack spontaneity, and the need to plan everything before acting can delay an opportunity that actually called for a faster response. What matters most here is learning that an unplanned action isn't necessarily a mistake — that there's real strength in spontaneous momentum too.",
    verseau: "Mars in Aquarius fights readily for collective causes, with an openly rebellious streak against authority and established convention. The friction shows up in self-interest: this energy, so easily mobilized for the collective, can struggle to show up just as fully for a purely individual goal, as if action needed a bigger justification than oneself to feel legitimate. The real work is learning that acting for one's own benefit isn't any less worthy than acting for a cause — that the two can coexist without contradiction.",
    poissons: "Mars in Pisces acts less head-on, guided by intuition rather than direct confrontation, with energy that prefers committing to idealistic causes over openly personal gain. The sensitive spot sits in self-assertion: this avoidance of direct conflict can turn into an inability to clearly defend one's own interests, letting energy dissolve into compromise or quiet renunciation. The lesson to take in is learning to act for oneself without needing a bigger cause to justify it — to allow a purely personal desire to be enough.",
  },
  jupiter: {
    belier: "Jupiter in Aries builds confidence through boldness: you grow by daring, by going first, with an almost innate conviction that taking the lead attracts luck. The shadow side lives in persistence: this early optimism can fade if results aren't immediate, and the expansion sought can stay stuck at the first burst of energy, never consolidated. What this placement asks for is learning that real growth is also measured by what's sustained over time, not just what's dared at the start.",
    taureau: "Jupiter in Taurus builds confidence through concrete accumulation: you grow by consolidating what already exists, with a keen sense of sensory and material abundance rather than abstract optimism. What costs the most plays out in excess: this natural generosity can slide into greed or accumulation for its own sake, a growth that gets confused with \"always more\" rather than genuine inner enrichment. The deeper task is learning that abundance isn't only measured in quantity — that one can grow by deepening what's already there, without needing to accumulate more.",
    gemeaux: "Jupiter in Gemini builds confidence through curiosity: you grow by multiplying exchanges, readings, points of view, convinced that every new piece of information genuinely widens the horizon. The knot sits in coherence: this accumulation of varied knowledge can stay superficial, a scattering of facts never woven into an actual overview. What's being cultivated here is learning to connect what's learned into a coherent thread, so curiosity becomes real wisdom rather than a collection of isolated facts.",
    cancer: "Jupiter in Cancer builds confidence through emotional connection: you grow by caring for others, by feeling like you belong to a protective circle, by giving without counting the cost to those you love. The main difficulty lies in limits: this emotional generosity can overflow into excessive protection or self-sacrifice for others, a growth that stops at the door of one's own family rather than opening up more broadly. The place to grow is learning that the same generosity can extend beyond the familiar circle without losing its warmth.",
    lion: "Jupiter in Leo builds a generous, radiant confidence: you grow by putting yourself out there, by creating, by betting on your own worth with an optimism that can look excessive from the outside. The downside shows up in measure: this self-confidence can slide into overestimation, an optimism that stops seeing real obstacles and takes disproportionate risks out of sheer self-assurance. The growth edge is learning to keep this radiance while still being able to hear a real limit without experiencing it as a challenge to one's worth.",
    vierge: "Jupiter in Virgo builds confidence through competence: you grow by perfecting a concrete skill, by making yourself useful, with a quality standard that can hold back this planet's naturally expansive energy. The tension lies in doubt: this attention to detail can prevent recognizing one's own progress, endlessly delaying the moment of allowing oneself to feel competent. What matters most here is learning to celebrate a success before it's perfect — to let confidence grow at the same pace as competence, not after it.",
    balance: "Jupiter in Libra builds confidence through relationship: you grow by partnering, by seeking balance and consensus, convinced that growth rarely happens in isolation. The friction shows up in autonomy: this need for partnership can hold back a decision that calls for going it alone, delaying an expansion that doesn't necessarily wait for everyone's agreement. The real work is learning that one can grow outside of consensus — that self-confidence doesn't always need the other's validation.",
    scorpion: "Jupiter in Scorpio builds confidence through intensity: you grow by going through deep ordeals rather than staying on the surface, convinced that what doesn't transform in depth only passes through without truly enriching. The sensitive spot sits in control: this growth-through-crisis pattern can create distrust of anything that comes easily, as if only hardship carried real value. The lesson to take in is learning that an opportunity can be legitimate even without having been hard-won.",
    sagittaire: "Jupiter is in its element here: confidence builds through an almost natural faith in the future, a thirst for meaning and wide horizons that makes optimism nearly instinctive. The shadow side lives in realism: this spontaneous confidence can underestimate real obstacles, promising more than can actually be delivered. What this placement asks for is learning to ground this faith in the future with a realistic check, without extinguishing the enthusiasm that makes it so valuable.",
    capricorne: "Jupiter in Capricorn builds confidence over time: you grow slowly but solidly, earning an authority through effort rather than receiving it for free. What costs the most plays out in the spontaneity of growth: this disciplined approach can hold back Jupiter's natural expansiveness, turning down an opportunity simply because it wasn't sufficiently earned or planned. The deeper task is learning that it's fine to receive a chance without having fully earned it beforehand.",
    verseau: "Jupiter in Aquarius builds confidence through deliberate difference: you grow by opening up to new ideas and causes larger than yourself, with a sense of belonging to something beyond the individual. The knot sits in personal grounding: this growth oriented toward the collective can neglect a more intimate, individual fulfillment, as if personal success alone were never enough to justify self-confidence. What's being cultivated here is learning that growing for oneself matters just as much as growing for a collective cause.",
    poissons: "Jupiter in Pisces builds confidence through surrender: you grow by trusting a movement larger than yourself, an intuition or spirituality that guides beyond rational control. The main difficulty lies in realism: this faith in the invisible can lose touch with concrete constraints, leading to generous but poorly grounded decisions. The place to grow is learning to keep one foot in the concrete while cultivating this intuitive trust — so that letting go stays a strength rather than becoming an escape.",
  },
  saturn: {
    belier: "Discipline is learned here through action: Saturn slows down Aries' natural impulsiveness, which can feel like frustration before it becomes a real strength of patience. The downside shows up in waiting: this brake can create a lasting sense of falling behind others, as if momentum always had to be earned or justified before it's allowed to unfold. The growth edge is learning that held-back action isn't missed action — that patience, once earned, becomes a real strategic asset over time.",
    taureau: "Saturn is particularly at home in Taurus: a natural sense of slow building, of solidity, of value that's earned rather than given. The tension lies in fear of loss: this hard-won solidity can make any change feel like a threat, even a positive one, to something that took so long to build. What matters most here is learning that a solid foundation can also evolve without collapsing.",
    gemeaux: "Discipline is learned here through intellectual rigor: Saturn channels Gemini's natural scatter into more structured knowledge, sometimes at the cost of spontaneity. The friction shows up in self-censorship: the fear of saying something wrong or seeming superficial can hold back a mind that actually needs to move freely to stay alive. The real work is learning that rigor and lightness of mind can coexist — that thought can be structured without losing its motion.",
    cancer: "Self-control is built here through the relationship to family and emotional security: often an early sense of responsibility, or an emotional reserve to move beyond. The sensitive spot sits in asking for help: having had to appear reliable so young often makes it hard, as an adult, to become someone who's cared for rather than someone who cares for everyone else. The lesson to take in is learning that being supported doesn't undo the responsibility one has proven able to carry.",
    lion: "Maturity develops here through the relationship to recognition: self-confidence has to be built step by step, often after doubting one's own worth more deeply than shows on the surface. The shadow side lives in comparison: this early doubt can lead to measuring one's worth against others rather than against a stable inner conviction. What this placement asks for is learning that recognition earned through so much effort eventually becomes a confidence that no longer depends on outside validation.",
    vierge: "The lesson settles in here through detail and demand: Virgo's natural perfectionism can turn into harshness toward oneself here, something to soften over time. What costs the most plays out in never feeling like enough: this doubled standard can turn every finished task into an occasion to spot what's still missing rather than what was actually achieved. The deeper task is learning to recognize work that's \"good enough\" without waiting for perfection to feel satisfied.",
    balance: "Structure takes hold here through relationship: measured commitment, a sharp sense of fairness, but also an initial fear of commitment to move past in order to build something solid with someone else. The knot sits in dreaded loneliness: this relational caution can delay a commitment that's actually wanted, out of fear of making the wrong choice or losing oneself in it. What's being cultivated here is learning that a commitment made seriously, even late, has full value — that caution doesn't rule out solidity.",
    scorpion: "Discipline is learned here through control and transformation: the need to master inner intensity rather than flee it or be overwhelmed by it. The main difficulty lies in letting go: this discipline of control can become a way of avoiding all vulnerability, as if losing control meant losing all security. The place to grow is learning that intensity, once channeled with discipline, can also be released sometimes without danger.",
    sagittaire: "Rigor takes shape here through belief: Saturn structures Sagittarius' enthusiasm, forcing it to concretely verify what it believes in. The downside shows up in existential doubt: this constant checking can shake a faith that actually needed a bit of naivety to stay alive and motivating. The growth edge is learning that a belief can be both seriously verified and enthusiastically lived, without one canceling the other.",
    capricorne: "Saturn is in its element here: an innate sense of responsibility, structure and patient ambition — often an early maturity that improves with age. The tension lies in lightness: this early-acquired maturity may have come at the cost of a carefree quality that others get to live without a second thought. What matters most here is learning, often late, to relax and play without feeling like it's wasting useful time.",
    verseau: "Self-control is built here through the relationship to the group and to difference: structuring one's singularity so it serves a collective project rather than staying marginal. The friction shows up in belonging: this structuring can feel like giving up part of one's difference, as if one had to choose between being oneself and being useful to the group. The real work is learning that singularity can be structured without being dissolved — it often becomes even more useful once channeled.",
    poissons: "Maturity develops here through the relationship to vagueness and letting go: giving concrete shape to intuition and sensitivity, without smothering them. The sensitive spot sits in rigidity: this search for structure, if pushed too far, can dry up the very sensitivity and intuition that made it rich in the first place. The lesson to take in is learning that a light structure can hold the vague rather than trying to eliminate it entirely.",
  },
  asc: {
    belier: "The Ascendant in Aries works like a suit of energetic armor: the first impression given is of someone ready to charge ahead, decide fast, go straight for the goal without detour or visible hesitation. The shadow side lives in what this quickness hides: this head-on approach can be a way of getting ahead of the world before it has a chance to strike first, a protective strategy disguised as confidence. What this placement asks for is learning that this energy doesn't need to stay permanently on the defensive to remain authentic.",
    taureau: "The Ascendant in Taurus works like an anchor: the calm presence, the slow pace, the solidity of bearing inspire trust and give the image of someone hard to shake. What costs the most plays out in change: this image of stability can become a role to maintain even when something is shifting inside that would actually need to be expressed. The deeper task is learning that showing hesitation or change doesn't crack the reassuring image — it makes it more human.",
    gemeaux: "The Ascendant in Gemini works like a social magnet: the quick, curious, talkative approach grabs attention fast, and the often young or mobile bearing gives an image of instant accessibility. The knot sits in perceived depth: this easy surface can give the impression of someone light or superficial, when it mostly serves to test the ground before opening up further. What's being cultivated here is learning to let what's behind the easy conversation show through, once trust is there.",
    cancer: "The Ascendant in Cancer works like a protective airlock: the soft, sensitive presence stays reserved at first contact, taking time to assess whether the environment is truly safe before opening up further. The main difficulty lies in the pace of trust: this initial caution can be misread as disinterest or coldness, when it's actually protecting a very real sensitivity. The place to grow is learning to signal, even discreetly, that the initial reserve isn't rejection but precaution.",
    lion: "The Ascendant in Leo works like a stage: the bearing doesn't go unnoticed, carried by presence, warmth and a natural flair for self-presentation that draws the eye without visible effort. The downside shows up in dependence on that gaze: this radiant image can become exhausting to maintain continuously, as if any dip in energy risked tarnishing the image projected. The growth edge is learning that showing tiredness or uncertainty doesn't dim the natural radiance being projected.",
    vierge: "The Ascendant in Virgo works like a card of competence: the understated, well-kept presence is often read as serious and reliable from the first contact, almost before anything's been proven. The tension lies in the pressure of that image: this reputation for competence, formed so fast, can make it hard to show hesitation or a mistake without fearing it will disappoint the image given. What matters most here is learning that visible imperfection doesn't destroy the trust it inspires — it often makes it feel closer.",
    balance: "The Ascendant in Libra works like an art of contact: the charming, courteous, aesthetically put-together approach immediately gives the image of someone who wants to build a pleasant relationship rather than a power struggle. The friction shows up in authenticity: this concern for making a good impression can turn into a facade that says what one thinks the other wants to hear, rather than what's actually meant. The real work is learning that a disagreement expressed with the same grace doesn't break the charm — it deepens it.",
    scorpion: "The Ascendant in Scorpio works like a half-drawn curtain: the magnetic, intense presence intrigues immediately, hinting at a depth that never fully reveals itself at first glance. The sensitive spot sits in approachability: this intensity can intimidate or hold others at a distance before any real contact has happened, unintentionally reinforcing a mysterious image that wasn't necessarily being cultivated on purpose. The lesson to take in is learning that one can appear approachable without losing the depth that makes the persona compelling.",
    sagittaire: "The Ascendant in Sagittarius works like an invitation to travel: the playful, frank, often exuberant bearing gives an immediate calling card built on humor and contagious optimism. The shadow side lives in perceived depth: this apparent lightness can lead others — and oneself — to underestimate the real substance or the real pain sometimes hiding behind the easy smile. What this placement asks for is learning to let real seriousness show through without fearing the loss of the lightness that remains a genuine strength.",
    capricorne: "The Ascendant in Capricorn works like a facade of solidity: the sober, reserved presence immediately inspires seriousness and reliability, sometimes read as distant before being better known. What costs the most plays out in hidden warmth: this initial reserve can make others miss a very real sensitivity and warmth that's rarely shown right away. The deeper task is learning that showing warmth earlier doesn't weaken the image of seriousness — it completes it.",
    verseau: "The Ascendant in Aquarius works like a declaration of independence: the original, slightly offbeat approach displays its difference without complex from the very first meeting, almost like an intentional calling card. The knot sits in belonging: this difference, asserted right away, can unintentionally hold others at a distance before a real bond has had time to form. What's being cultivated here is learning that showing closeness to others, even briefly, doesn't erase the singularity so proudly displayed.",
    poissons: "The Ascendant in Pisces works like a soft veil: the gentle, dreamy presence often seems to be elsewhere, hard to pin down, as if part of the person always stayed slightly outside the present moment. The main difficulty lies in legibility: this porous bearing can give a blurred image, hard to read, that others sometimes interpret their own way rather than as it actually is. The place to grow is learning to give this presence a clearer outline from time to time, without sacrificing the softness that defines it.",
  },
};
